To generate an automatic table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document, you can use the `toc` package along with some custom commands to create the TOC structure and add links to each section. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}
\usepackage[english]{babel}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}

% Custom command for adding sections to the TOC
\newcommand{\addsection}[2]{
  \setcounter{secnumdepth}{0} % Set toc depth to zero
  \renewcommand*\contentsname{Table of Contents}
  \begin{verse}
    \textbf{#1}\vspace{-3pt}
    \textit{#2}
  \end{verse}
}

% Main content of the document
\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

% Add sections to the TOC
\addsection{Introduction}
\addsection{Background}
\addsection{Methodology}
\addsection{Results}
\addsection{Discussion}
\addsection{Conclusion}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we first import the `toc` package and set up the font encoding and language settings. Then, we define a new command `\addsection` that adds a section to the TOC using the `verse` environment from the `amsmath` and `amssymb` packages. The `\setcounter{secnumdepth}{0}` command sets the toc depth to zero so that it doesn't affect the number of sections in the TOC.

Next, we include our main content of the document by setting up the `toctree` option in the `document` environment. We then call the `\addsection` command for each section in the TOC, passing in the title and description as arguments.

Finally, we close the `toctree` environment and print out the TOC using the `\tableofcontents` command.

When you run this code, you should see a TOC with three sections: Introduction, Background, Methodology, Results, Discussion, and Conclusion. Each section will have its own link to the corresponding section in the main text of the document.

